# Secured and Monitored Web Infrastructure

This setup shows how to build a secure website infrastructure for www.foobar.com using three servers with proper security and monitoring.

## What we have here:

User visits website -> Goes through security firewall -> Reaches load balancer -> Gets distributed to one of two web servers -> Each server has its own database

Everything is watched by monitoring tools to make sure it works properly.

## The Setup

**Security stuff:**
- 3 firewalls protecting different parts
- SSL certificate so the website uses HTTPS

**Main server:**
- Load balancer (HAproxy) that spreads traffic around

**Two web servers, each with:**
- Nginx web server
- Application code
- MySQL database
- Monitoring tool watching everything

**Domain setup:**
- www.foobar.com points to our load balancer at 8.8.8.8

## How it works when someone visits the site:

1. Person types www.foobar.com in their browser
2. Browser connects using HTTPS (secure connection)
3. Request hits our first firewall protecting the load balancer
4. Load balancer receives the request and picks which web server to use
5. Request goes through another firewall to reach the chosen web server
6. Web server processes the request and talks to its database if needed
7. Response goes back the same way to the user

## Why we added each new thing:

**Three Firewalls:**
We put firewalls in three places because each one protects a different part of our system. The first one guards our load balancer from bad traffic coming from the internet. The other two protect each web server from getting attacked directly. Think of them like security guards at different doors.

**SSL Certificate (HTTPS):**
This makes sure all the data between users and our website is encrypted. Without it, anyone could spy on what people are doing on our site. It also makes browsers show a lock icon so users trust our site more. Google also ranks HTTPS sites higher in search results.

**Three Monitoring Clients:**
These are like having security cameras everywhere. One watches the load balancer to see how traffic is flowing. The other two watch each web server to make sure they're running smoothly. They collect information about how fast things are running, if there are any errors, and if something is about to break.

## What firewalls actually do:

Firewalls are like bouncers at a club. They check every piece of network traffic trying to get in or out of our servers. They have rules about what's allowed and what's not. For example, they might block traffic from known bad IP addresses or only allow web traffic on ports 80 and 443. They also stop hackers from scanning our servers to find weaknesses.

## Why we use HTTPS:

When you visit a regular HTTP website, everything you do is sent in plain text. Anyone between you and the website can read your passwords, personal info, or anything else you type. HTTPS encrypts all this data so only you and the website can understand it. It also proves that you're actually talking to the real website and not some fake one set up by hackers.

## What monitoring is for:

Monitoring helps us know what's happening with our website all the time. It tells us things like:
- How many people are visiting right now
- If any servers are running out of memory or disk space
- How fast pages are loading
- If there are any error messages users are seeing
- If someone might be attacking our site

This way we can fix problems before users even notice them.

## How the monitoring tools collect data:

The monitoring agents are small programs installed on each server. They constantly check things like CPU usage, memory, disk space, and network activity. They also read log files to see what the web server and database are doing. All this information gets sent securely to a central monitoring service (like Sumologic) where we can see charts and get alerts if something goes wrong.

## Monitoring web server QPS (requests per second):

If you want to see how many requests per second your web server is handling:

1. Turn on Nginx's status page by adding this to the config:
```
location /nginx_status {
    stub_status on;
    allow 127.0.0.1;
    deny all;
}
```

2. Set up your monitoring tool to check this page every minute
3. Have it count the requests and calculate how many happen per second
4. Set up alerts so you know if traffic gets unusually high or low
5. You can also track requests at the load balancer level to see the total traffic

## Problems with this setup:

**SSL termination at the load balancer:**
Right now, traffic is encrypted from users to the load balancer, but then it becomes unencrypted between the load balancer and web servers. This means if someone gains access to our internal network, they could spy on this traffic. Also, the load balancer has to do all the work of encrypting and decrypting, which can slow things down.

**Only one database can handle writes:**
Even though we have two databases, they're set up so only one can accept new data or changes at a time. If that database goes down, users can't create accounts, make purchases, or do anything that involves saving data. The whole site basically becomes read-only until we fix it.

**Every server has everything on it:**
Both web servers are running a web server, application code, AND a database all on the same machine. This is wasteful because:
- The database might not get used much but still takes up memory
- If we need more web capacity, we have to also add more database capacity we don't need
- If one service crashes, it might affect the others
- It's harder to figure out what's causing problems
- Updates become more complicated because changing one thing might break another

A better approach would be to separate these into different servers - some just for web serving, some just for applications, and some just for databases. That way each can be optimized for its specific job and scaled independently.